{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-95874",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x={cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+{1}^{2}]-[\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡1+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
